From 85e63272fa704420c16297cd61c02bb87a946606 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Claude (draft)" <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 16:53:48 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/9] mm/page_alloc: don't apply watermark boost to the min
 watermark

When a fallback steals a pageblock of another migratetype,
boost_watermark() raises zone->watermark_boost by a pageblock per event,
up to watermark_boost_factor (default 150%) of the high watermark.
wmark_pages() adds the boost to every watermark, including WMARK_MIN,
so ordinary allocations that are well above the real min watermark
fail the slow path check and enter direct reclaim (allocstall) until
kswapd has worked the boost off. Only order-0 __GFP_HIGH allocations
are currently exempt.

With a raised vm.watermark_scale_factor the high watermark, and hence
the maximum boost, gets large (several GB on a 32 GB machine), which
makes this a noticeable source of foreground stalls.

The purpose of the boost is to make kswapd reclaim ahead of future
fallbacks; rmqueue() already wakes kswapd when the zone is boosted and
the boosted low/high watermarks keep it running. Ignore the boost for
all WMARK_MIN checks in zone_watermark_fast().

throttle_direct_reclaim() has the same problem one step later. It puts
a task that enters direct reclaim to sleep while free memory in the
zones up to ZONE_NORMAL is at or below half of the "pfmemalloc reserve",
and allow_direct_reclaim() sums that reserve with min_wmark_pages(),
which includes zone->watermark_boost. With the change above, ordinary
allocations enter direct reclaim only below the real (unboosted) min
watermark. Whenever the boost of a zone is larger than its min
watermark, half of (min + boost) is above min, so a task entering
direct reclaim while a boost is active would be throttled until kswapd
has lifted free memory above that line (sleeping without timeout for
__GFP_FS requests, up to 1 s otherwise). pgscan_direct_throttle in
/proc/vmstat counts these. Use the unboosted min watermark for the
reserve as well.

The throttle part is compile-tested only (7.3.4 and 7.4-rc1).

DRAFT for linux-lunar; not submitted upstream. Test status: see
the linux-lunar README.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Jpc2uj1P9if4WYVKq5YGZK
Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01HhEc6jiSB1p6Eb6tsXUo21
---
 mm/page_alloc.c | 17 ++++++++++-------
 mm/vmscan.c     |  7 ++++---
 2 files changed, 14 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

diff --git a/mm/page_alloc.c b/mm/page_alloc.c
index 12fac9084..1f9a0a03b 100644
--- a/mm/page_alloc.c
+++ b/mm/page_alloc.c
@@ -3710,13 +3710,16 @@ static inline bool zone_watermark_fast(struct zone *z, unsigned int order,
 		return true;
 
 	/*
-	 * Ignore watermark boosting for __GFP_HIGH order-0 allocations
-	 * when checking the min watermark. The min watermark is the
-	 * point where boosting is ignored so that kswapd is woken up
-	 * when below the low watermark.
-	 */
-	if (unlikely(!order && (alloc_flags & ALLOC_MIN_RESERVE) && z->watermark_boost
-		&& ((alloc_flags & ALLOC_WMARK_MASK) == WMARK_MIN))) {
+	 * Ignore watermark boosting when checking the min watermark.
+	 * The boost exists to make kswapd reclaim ahead of a fragmentation
+	 * event (it raises low/high, and rmqueue() wakes kswapd when it
+	 * is set). Applying it to the min watermark as well pushes
+	 * allocations that are comfortably above the real min watermark
+	 * into direct reclaim, i.e. it converts a background defrag hint
+	 * into foreground allocation stalls.
+	 */
+	if (unlikely(z->watermark_boost &&
+		     ((alloc_flags & ALLOC_WMARK_MASK) == WMARK_MIN))) {
 		mark = z->_watermark[WMARK_MIN];
 		return __zone_watermark_ok(z, order, mark, highest_zoneidx,
 					alloc_flags, free_pages);
diff --git a/mm/vmscan.c b/mm/vmscan.c
index f11491ee9..bdd5f4174 100644
--- a/mm/vmscan.c
+++ b/mm/vmscan.c
@@ -6656,7 +6656,7 @@ static bool allow_direct_reclaim(pg_data_t *pgdat)
 		if (!zone_reclaimable_pages(zone) && zone_page_state_snapshot(zone, NR_FREE_PAGES))
 			continue;
 
-		pfmemalloc_reserve += min_wmark_pages(zone);
+		pfmemalloc_reserve += zone->_watermark[WMARK_MIN];
 		free_pages += zone_page_state_snapshot(zone, NR_FREE_PAGES);
 	}
 
@@ -7180,8 +7180,9 @@ static int balance_pgdat(pg_data_t *pgdat, int order, int highest_zoneidx)
 
 	/*
 	 * Account for the reclaim boost. Note that the zone boost is left in
-	 * place so that parallel allocations that are near the watermark will
-	 * stall or direct reclaim until kswapd is finished.
+	 * place so that kswapd keeps being woken while it works; it no longer
+	 * applies to the min watermark, so it does not push parallel
+	 * allocations into direct reclaim (see zone_watermark_fast()).
 	 */
 	nr_boost_reclaim = 0;
 	for_each_managed_zone_pgdat(zone, pgdat, i, highest_zoneidx) {
-- 
